# DNS Daddy — Docker Compose environment.
#
#   ./deploy/install-docker.sh
#
# The installer creates this file for you, so you should not normally need to
# copy it by hand. Every value here is optional: docker-compose.yml and the
# binary carry working defaults, and this file is for what is specific to your
# deployment.
#
# .env is gitignored: it holds addresses specific to your deployment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Forward first, then choose Learn or Live
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A new installation forwards through Cloudflare DoH on TCP 443 by default.
# Overview and Daddybound offer Forward, Learn and Live; select a mode and
# Apply resolver mode. Existing saved choices survive upgrades. If a previous
# Live setup fails to resolve, choose Forward explicitly to test forwarding.
# Forward keeps filtering and access controls, but does not validate DNSSEC
# locally. Native Learn/Live also need direct outbound UDP/TCP 53.
#
# For the encrypted profile, which also encrypts supporting and background DNS:
# Dashboard: Daddybound -> DNS transport -> Encrypted forwarding ->
# Add Cloudflare example. Review the endpoints and agreement, Test endpoints,
# then Apply DNS transport. The example only fills the form and preserves your
# existing Forward/Learn/Live mode. Verify real answers before choosing Live.
# Leave these variables commented out to retain settings saved in the UI.
#
# For a configuration-managed example, run from the repository root:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f deploy/docker-compose.encrypted.yml up -d --build
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f deploy/docker-compose.encrypted.yml exec dnsdaddy dnsdaddy doctor
# This mounts dnsdaddy.encrypted.example.yaml. Read it to see the TLS name,
# literal bootstrap IPs and provider choice. Use both -f arguments on updates.
#
# Alternatively, put BOTH lines below in .env and recreate the service.
# This example explicitly chooses Cloudflare DoH over TCP 443. The literal IPs
# avoid needing working plaintext DNS to connect to the encrypted provider;
# TLS still verifies cloudflare-dns.com. No plaintext fallback is enabled.
# DNSDADDY_RESOLUTION_TRANSPORT=encrypted
# DNSDADDY_ENCRYPTED_UPSTREAMS='[{"protocol":"doh2","address":"https://cloudflare-dns.com/dns-query","serverName":"cloudflare-dns.com","bootstrapIPs":["1.1.1.1","1.0.0.1"]}]'
#
#   docker compose up -d --build
#   docker compose exec dnsdaddy dnsdaddy doctor
#
# Do not set encrypted transport on its own: it needs approved endpoints.
# Explicit environment settings override YAML and pin their dashboard controls.
# To return transport control to the dashboard, comment both lines out and run
# docker compose up -d again; the existing saved settings remain available.
# Resolver mode remains editable because it is not pinned by this example.
# To deliberately pin it instead, set one of off / observe / enforce (the UI
# names are Forward / Learn / Live). Omit this to retain the saved UI choice:
# DNSDADDY_LOCAL_DNSSEC_VALIDATION=off
#
# This encrypts DNS Daddy's outbound DNS, including Daddybound validation.
# Client-to-server DNS on port 53 is a separate hop; use the tokenized DoH URL
# over HTTPS shown on Setup for encrypted clients. Public client addresses
# still require permission under Networks, as explained below.
#
# Forwarders used by native-transport Forward/Learn can also be overridden.
# These are the built-in defaults; a custom Pi-hole address can be used instead:
# DNSDADDY_UPSTREAMS=https://1.1.1.1/dns-query,https://1.0.0.1/dns-query
# DNSDADDY_UPSTREAM_MODE=failover

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who may use this resolver
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Normal setup: in Networks, add the actual client source address or subnet,
# enable the Network and tick "Allow this network to use DNS Daddy". This
# applies to LAN and public clients. It takes effect on the next query.
# Test inside the container first: docker compose exec dnsdaddy dnsdaddy doctor
# Then test from the permitted client before changing your router's DNS.
#
# A host-side dig @127.0.0.1 crosses Docker NAT: the container can see its
# project gateway instead of loopback. If you want to use that host probe,
# add the actual gateway as an enabled permitted /32 Network. Find it with:
#   docker inspect dnsdaddy --format '{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{println .Gateway}}{{end}}'
# An in-container doctor uses real loopback and avoids that ambiguity.
#
# DNSDADDY_ALLOWED_CLIENT_CIDRS is the bootstrap pool. Its non-loopback ranges
# are used only when ad-hoc access is enabled; the default ad-hoc gate is OFF.
# Setting this variable alone does not bypass that gate. Named, permitted
# Networks grant access independently. Doctor shows the effective result.
#
# Normally leave this commented out and use Networks. The built-in pool
# covers loopback, private ranges, carrier-grade NAT, link-local and IPv6
# equivalents. Setting the variable REPLACES that pool; keep loopback for
# local checks. When ad-hoc access is deliberately enabled, unchecking a
# Network does not revoke a range still permitted by the bootstrap pool.
#
# Example bootstrap pool for two sites (requires deliberate ad-hoc access):
# DNSDADDY_ALLOWED_CLIENT_CIDRS=127.0.0.0/8,172.16.0.0/12,203.0.113.42/32,198.51.100.10/32
#
# Whatever you set, `dnsdaddy doctor` will tell you whether the networks in
# your dashboard can actually reach the resolver.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reaching the dashboard
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# By default the dashboard is published on 127.0.0.1:8080 — reachable from the
# server itself and nowhere else.
#
# ON A MACHINE WITH NO PUBLIC ADDRESS — a home server, a LAN VM, a box behind
# your router — run `./deploy/install-docker.sh --lan`, which confirms the
# address with you and sets this line. Or set it yourself:
#
# DNSDADDY_DASHBOARD_BIND=192.168.1.75
#
# CHECK THIS IS NOT A CLOUD VPS FIRST. On AWS, GCP, Azure, Hetzner Cloud and
# most providers the instance holds a private address and the public one is
# NATed onto it, so `ip addr` shows 10.x or 172.x while the machine is fully
# reachable from the internet. Binding the dashboard to that private address
# publishes an authenticated but *plaintext* management API to the world. A
# private address on the NIC is not evidence that a host is private.
#
# Do NOT set this to 0.0.0.0 either, and note that Docker's port publishing
# bypasses ufw so a firewall rule will not contain it. On any host with a
# public address, leave this alone and use an SSH tunnel or put TLS in front —
# see the reverse proxy section below.
#
# If it does go wrong, DNS Daddy says so: a management request arriving over
# plain HTTP from a public address is recorded and raised on the dashboard and
# at /api/v1/diagnostics.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reverse proxy (leave blank if the dashboard is loopback-only with no proxy)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Peers whose X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP / X-Forwarded-Proto are believed.
#
# Counter-intuitive but important: with Caddy on the host proxying to
# 127.0.0.1:8080, the container does NOT see 127.0.0.1. Docker's port
# publishing rewrites the source to the container's project-network gateway.
# Trust that host address, not Docker's unrelated global "bridge" network.
# The --https installer discovers and configures it after the container starts.
#
# Find yours:  docker inspect dnsdaddy --format '{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{println .Gateway}}{{end}}'
# Example if the output is 172.18.0.1:
# DNSDADDY_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=172.18.0.1/32

# Session cookie Secure flag: auto | always | never.
# Set "always" once TLS is in front, so it can never cross plain HTTP.
# DNSDADDY_SECURE_COOKIES=always

# Public URL, used to build the DoH URLs shown on the Setup page.
# DNSDADDY_BASE_URL=https://dns.example.co.uk

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Behavioural detection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On by default. Nothing here blocks anything — the detectors observe, score,
# explain and alert; enforcement stays with policy and threat feeds. Every
# detector is experimental. See docs/detection/README.md.
#
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_ENABLED=true

# info | low | medium | high. Findings below this are not stored.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_MIN_SEVERITY=low

# Write findings as newline-delimited JSON for a log shipper to tail. A
# relative path lands inside the data volume. Off by default: this is browsing
# history in a second place. See docs/siem.md.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_FINDINGS_FILE=findings.jsonl

# Parent domains exempt from behavioural findings, on top of the built-in list.
# Reputation services, CDNs and mail gateways legitimately produce traffic
# shaped exactly like a DNS tunnel. This never affects whether a domain
# resolves.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_EXCLUDED_DOMAINS=reputation.your-vendor.example,gateway.your-filter.example

# How long findings are kept. They outlive the query log on purpose.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Threat feeds
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The DNS Daddy Threat Observatory (threats.dnsdaddy.dev) ships in the catalog
# DISABLED, so a stock install fetches nothing from a server we run. Enable it
# on the Threat feeds page if you want it; docs/threat-intel.md says exactly
# what that exposes (your server's IP and refresh cadence, never your queries).
# There is nothing to configure here: what it blocks is decided by the
# categories your policies enable, like every other feed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TZ=UTC
# DNSDADDY_VERSION=dev
